/* WISEBYTE LESSONS · Psychology: manipulation and quirks of the mind */
WB.L("manipulation-tactics", "psychology", "beginner", "How manipulators work",
  "Manipulation works because it's hard to see from the inside. Learn the common tactics and they lose much of their power.",
  [
    ["Influence versus manipulation", "Persuasion is open and respects your choice; manipulation is hidden and serves the manipulator.",
      "We all try to influence each other: asking for a favour, making a case, sharing an opinion. Manipulation is different. It works by exploiting emotions, hiding intentions or distorting information so that you act against your own interests or judgement. A useful test: would the tactic still work if you fully understood what was happening? Honest persuasion usually would, because it relies on reasons you can inspect. Manipulation usually wouldn't, because it relies on you not noticing. Most people use a little pressure now and then. What marks a manipulator is a repeated pattern of it, used to get their way at your expense."],
    ["Guilt, fear and obligation", "Manipulators often use guilt, fear and a sense of obligation, sometimes called emotional blackmail.",
      "Therapist Susan Forward, in her 1997 book Emotional Blackmail, described the tactic as using FOG: fear, obligation and guilt. 'After everything I've done for you…' creates obligation. 'If you leave, I don't know what I'll do' uses fear and guilt. The goal is to make saying no feel impossibly costly, so you give in to avoid the emotional fallout. Healthy relationships involve give and take, and everyone sometimes feels guilty for good reason. The warning sign is when your choices are regularly steered by threats of sulking, anger, withdrawal or punishment, rather than by honest conversation about what each person needs."],
    ["Love bombing", "Love bombing is overwhelming someone with affection early on to create attachment and dependence.",
      "Love bombing means intense flattery, gifts, constant contact and talk of destiny, very early in a relationship. The term was first used in the 1970s to describe how some cults welcomed recruits, and it later spread to romantic relationships. It feels wonderful, which is the point: it creates a strong bond quickly, before you know the person well enough to judge them. Later, affection may be withdrawn and used as leverage, leaving you chasing the early high. Genuine affection grows at a reasonable pace, and it respects your independence, your friendships and your need for time to decide."],
    ["Pressure and false urgency", "Creating artificial urgency stops people thinking carefully.",
      "'This offer ends tonight.' 'Decide now or lose it.' 'If you really loved me, you wouldn't need to think about it.' Pressure tactics work because careful thinking takes time, and manipulators don't want you to take it. Under time pressure, people lean on quick emotional reactions rather than weighing evidence. Scammers, high-pressure salespeople and controlling partners all use urgency, and many phone and text scams in Australia claim a bank account, parcel or tax debt needs action within hours. A simple defence is a personal rule: for any significant decision, you'll sleep on it. Legitimate offers and relationships will survive a day's wait."],
    ["Denial and blame-shifting", "When confronted, manipulators often deny, attack and recast themselves as the victim.",
      "Psychologist Jennifer Freyd described a common response to being held accountable, which she called DARVO: deny, attack, and reverse victim and offender. You raise a genuine concern, and within minutes you're the one apologising. Related moves include changing the subject, bringing up your past mistakes, or claiming you're 'too sensitive'. The effect is that the original issue never gets resolved. A helpful habit is to notice where a conversation ends up. If raising any problem reliably turns into a discussion of your faults, that pattern is worth taking seriously, whatever the other person's explanation."],
    ["Isolation and divide-and-conquer", "Controlling people often cut targets off from friends and family who might question them.",
      "One of the most serious warning signs is someone gradually isolating you: criticising your friends, making it awkward to see family, sulking when you go out, or monitoring your phone. Isolation removes outside perspectives that might say 'this isn't normal', and it increases your dependence on the manipulator. It rarely happens all at once; it usually creeps in as small complaints and inconveniences. Manipulators may also play people against each other, telling different stories to different people, a tactic often called triangulation. Staying connected to trusted people, even when it's made difficult, is one of the best protections you have."],
    ["When it becomes abuse", "A pattern of controlling behaviour, known as coercive control, is a recognised form of domestic abuse.",
      "Manipulation can shade into abuse. Coercive control describes an ongoing pattern of behaviour that takes away a person's freedom: tracking their movements, controlling money, restricting who they see, making threats, and wearing down their confidence. It often involves no physical violence at all, which is why it can go unrecognised for years. Research on domestic homicides has found that coercive control frequently precedes physical violence. New South Wales made coercive control in current and former intimate relationships a criminal offence from July 2024, Queensland's similar offence took effect in 2025, and South Australia has also legislated. Other jurisdictions are developing their own approaches."],
    ["Protecting yourself", "Name the tactic, slow down, check with trusted people, and set boundaries.",
      "Recognising a tactic is powerful: once you think 'this is false urgency', the pressure fades. Slow decisions down. Talk things over with someone outside the situation. Notice patterns rather than single incidents, and consider keeping a private note of what happens. You can say no without a long justification. If someone's behaviour includes threats, control or fear, it may be abuse, and in Australia you can contact 1800RESPECT (1800 737 732), a free, confidential service available 24 hours a day, for advice about your situation or someone else's. In an emergency, or if you're in immediate danger, call 000."],
  ],
  [
    [0, "What's a useful test for manipulation?", ["Would it still work if you fully understood it?", "Is it polite?", "Is it fast?", "Is it free?"], 0, "Honest persuasion usually survives full understanding."],
    [1, "What does FOG stand for in emotional blackmail?", ["Force, order, greed", "Fear, obligation and guilt", "Flattery, orders, gifts", "Fame, opinion, gossip"], 1, "Described by Susan Forward."],
    [2, "What is love bombing?", ["Breaking up by text", "Overwhelming affection early on to create dependence", "A dating app", "A romantic gesture after years"], 1, "Affection may later be used as leverage."],
    [3, "What's a good defence against false urgency?", ["Decide immediately", "A rule to sleep on big decisions", "Ask for a discount", "Trust your gut"], 1, "Legitimate offers survive a day's wait."],
    [5, "Why do controlling people isolate their targets?", ["To save money", "To remove outside perspectives that might question them", "For privacy", "It's accidental"], 1, "Stay connected to trusted people."],
  ]);

WB.L("gaslighting", "psychology", "intermediate", "Gaslighting",
  "Making someone doubt their own memory and sanity. Where the word comes from, how it works, and how to keep your footing.",
  [
    ["Where the word comes from", "The term comes from the 1938 play Gas Light, in which a husband makes his wife doubt her sanity.",
      "In Patrick Hamilton's 1938 play Gas Light, and the 1944 film Gaslight starring Ingrid Bergman, a husband secretly dims the house's gas lights and hides objects, then insists his wife is imagining things. He does it to hide his own crimes and keep control of her. Slowly she starts to believe she's losing her mind. The word gaslighting now describes a pattern of manipulation that makes someone doubt their own perceptions, memory or judgement. It moved from therapists' offices into everyday speech, and was so widely searched that Merriam-Webster named it word of the year in 2022."],
    ["How it works", "Gaslighting relies on repeated denial, contradiction and rewriting of events.",
      "Common tactics include flatly denying things that happened ('I never said that'), trivialising feelings ('You're too sensitive'), countering memories ('That's not how it happened, you always get things wrong'), and shifting blame. Some gaslighters mix these with warmth and kindness, which makes the target less likely to trust their own doubts. Others tell friends and family that the target is 'confused' or 'unstable', so outside support weakens. Over time, the target starts to trust the gaslighter's version of reality over their own. It often works gradually, so each incident seems small. The pattern, repeated over months, is what does the damage."],
    ["Why it's effective", "Memory is imperfect, so a confident, repeated denial can genuinely create doubt.",
      "Everyone's memory is fallible, and we know it, which is exactly why gaslighting works. Psychology research shows memories are rebuilt each time we recall them and can be nudged by other people's accounts. So when someone we trust confidently insists we're wrong, again and again, it's natural to doubt ourselves. Gaslighting is especially powerful in relationships with an imbalance of power or deep emotional dependence: partners, parents, bosses. The more isolated someone is, the fewer people they have to check reality with, and the more the gaslighter's version becomes the only one they hear."],
    ["What gaslighting isn't", "Honest disagreement about what happened is not gaslighting.",
      "The word is now used loosely. Two people remembering an argument differently, or someone disagreeing with you, isn't gaslighting. Nor is being corrected when you genuinely made a mistake, or someone lying once to get out of trouble. Gaslighting is a pattern of deliberately or habitually undermining someone's grip on reality in a way that gives the other person control. A useful question is whether the disagreements consistently leave one person more confused, more dependent and less confident. Using the term too casually can make it harder to recognise, and take seriously, when it really happens."],
    ["Not just couples", "Gaslighting can happen in families, workplaces and other settings with a power imbalance.",
      "Gaslighting is most often discussed in romantic relationships, but the same pattern appears elsewhere. A parent may repeatedly tell a child their feelings or memories are wrong. A manager may deny giving instructions, then blame an employee for following them, or insist a problem the employee raised never happened. Some writers also use the term for institutions that deny well-documented problems, though that use is broader and more debated. The common thread is the same: one party has more power, and uses it to override another person's account of their own experience until they stop trusting it."],
    ["The effects", "Long-term gaslighting can cause anxiety, confusion, low self-esteem and dependence.",
      "People who've been gaslit over time often describe constantly second-guessing themselves, apologising excessively, feeling confused, and struggling to make even small decisions. They may withdraw from friends and rely more on the very person undermining them. Some say the hardest part to recover from is not trusting their own mind. Gaslighting is recognised as a common feature of coercive control, a pattern of domestic abuse. That is one reason abuse is increasingly understood as more than physical violence: a sustained attack on someone's sense of reality can do lasting harm."],
    ["Supporting someone else", "Believe them, stay connected, and gently offer your own view of events rather than lecturing.",
      "If you think a friend or relative is being gaslit, the most useful thing is often simply to stay in their life. Listen without judging, and tell them what you notice, calmly and specifically: 'I was there, and I remember it the way you do.' That outside reality check is exactly what gaslighting tries to remove. Avoid ultimatums or attacking their partner, which can push them to defend the relationship. Let them make their own decisions, and make sure they know where help is. Services like 1800RESPECT also advise friends and family, not only the person directly affected."],
    ["Keeping your footing", "Keep records, check with trusted people, and trust patterns over single moments.",
      "If you suspect gaslighting, write things down with dates soon after they happen, or keep messages and emails, so you have a record to trust. Store them somewhere safe that the other person can't access. Talk to people outside the relationship who can offer a reality check. Notice patterns, such as always ending up apologising. You don't need to win every argument about what happened; you can simply say 'I remember it differently' and leave it. Counsellors can help you rebuild confidence, and 1800RESPECT (1800 737 732) offers free, confidential support in Australia. In an emergency, call 000."],
  ],
  [
    [0, "Where does the word 'gaslighting' come from?", ["A gas company", "A 1938 play in which a husband makes his wife doubt her sanity", "Victorian street lamps", "A psychology textbook"], 1, "Later a 1944 film."],
    [1, "Which is a gaslighting tactic?", ["Apologising sincerely", "Flatly denying things that happened", "Giving a gift", "Asking for help"], 1, "Along with trivialising feelings."],
    [2, "Why does gaslighting work?", ["People are foolish", "Memory is imperfect, so confident repeated denial creates doubt", "It uses hypnosis", "It's quick"], 1, "Isolation makes it stronger."],
    [3, "Is remembering an argument differently gaslighting?", ["Always", "No, honest disagreement isn't gaslighting", "Only if you're angry", "Only in marriages"], 1, "Gaslighting is a pattern aimed at control."],
    [7, "What helps if you suspect gaslighting?", ["Confronting them loudly", "Keeping dated records and checking with trusted people", "Ignoring it", "Agreeing with them"], 1, "Trust patterns over single moments."],
  ]);

WB.L("dark-triad", "psychology", "intermediate", "The dark triad",
  "Narcissism, Machiavellianism and psychopathy: the three 'dark' personality traits psychologists study, and why they're more common than you'd think.",
  [
    ["Three dark traits", "The dark triad is narcissism, Machiavellianism and psychopathy, three overlapping 'dark' traits.",
      "In 2002, psychologists Delroy Paulhus and Kevin Williams, at the University of British Columbia, grouped three traits that share a core of callousness and self-interest. Importantly, in this research they're measured as traits everyone has to some degree, not clinical diagnoses. Most people score low to moderate, and a high score doesn't make someone a criminal or a monster. The three traits overlap but aren't identical. People high on them tend to be charming at first, exploit others when it suits them, and care little about the harm they cause. The idea caught on quickly and has since generated thousands of studies."],
    ["Narcissism", "Narcissism involves grandiosity, entitlement and a hunger for admiration.",
      "Named after Narcissus, the youth in Greek myth who fell in love with his own reflection, narcissism involves an inflated sense of self-importance, a need for admiration and a sense of entitlement. High scorers often make great first impressions: confident, entertaining, charismatic. Over time, their lack of empathy and strong reactions to criticism can damage relationships. Researchers also distinguish grandiose narcissism, which is bold and attention-seeking, from vulnerable narcissism, which is more anxious, resentful and easily wounded. Narcissistic personality disorder is a separate clinical diagnosis, made only by professionals, and much rarer than the everyday trait."],
    ["Machiavellianism", "Machiavellianism is cynical, strategic manipulation of others for personal gain.",
      "This trait is named after Niccolò Machiavelli, the Florentine writer whose book The Prince, published in 1532, argued that if a ruler can't be both, it is safer to be feared than loved, and that rulers must sometimes act without mercy or honesty. People high in Machiavellianism see others as tools, are cynical about human nature, and are willing to deceive and flatter strategically. Psychologists Richard Christie and Florence Geis built a questionnaire to measure it around 1970. Unlike psychopathy, it's usually described as more calculating and long-term than impulsive, though some researchers question how distinct the two really are."],
    ["Psychopathy", "Psychopathic traits include lack of empathy and remorse, impulsivity and thrill-seeking.",
      "In this research, psychopathy means a set of traits: shallow emotions, little empathy or guilt, impulsiveness and a hunger for thrills. Of the three, it's generally considered the darkest, most linked to antisocial behaviour. Clinical psychopathy, assessed by trained professionals with tools like Canadian psychologist Robert Hare's checklist, is rare, commonly estimated at around 1% of the general population, though much higher in prisons. Psychopathy isn't itself a formal diagnosis in the main psychiatric manuals. Many people with some psychopathic traits never commit crimes, and some research suggests these traits are more common in certain high-pressure leadership roles, though findings vary."],
    ["How they're measured", "Researchers mostly use short self-report questionnaires, which have real limitations.",
      "Most dark triad research uses questionnaires where people rate statements about themselves, such as the 27-item Short Dark Triad or an even briefer 12-item scale nicknamed the Dirty Dozen. These are quick and cheap, which helped the field grow fast, but they have limits. People may not answer honestly, especially about traits linked to deceit. Very short scales can blur important differences between traits. And critics note that Machiavellianism and psychopathy scores often correlate so strongly that they may partly measure the same thing. Findings based on these tools are best read as broad patterns, not precise portraits of individuals."],
    ["Why they often succeed", "Dark triad traits can help in the short term, through charm, confidence and risk-taking.",
      "Confidence and charisma get people noticed, promoted and chosen as leaders, especially at first. Studies suggest people higher on dark traits may pursue more short-term relationships and sometimes get ahead in competitive workplaces, particularly where they're judged on quick impressions. The costs tend to show up over time: broken trust, high staff turnover, toxic teams, and ethical or financial scandals. Researchers have since proposed adding a fourth trait, everyday sadism, meaning enjoyment of others' suffering, to make a 'dark tetrad'. Others have suggested a single underlying 'dark core' of putting one's own interests ahead of others'."],
    ["Where the traits come from", "Genes, upbringing and life experience all shape dark traits, and they can shift with age.",
      "Like most personality traits, dark traits seem to arise from a mix of genes and environment. Twin studies suggest genes play a moderate role, with upbringing, peers and life experiences also mattering. Harsh or neglectful environments may encourage a more exploitative, 'look after yourself' strategy. The traits aren't fixed for life either: studies following people over years suggest narcissism, on average, tends to decline from young adulthood into middle age. This matters because it pushes against the idea that some people are simply born bad. Dark traits are tendencies on a spectrum, shaped by circumstances, not a separate species of human."],
    ["Protecting yourself", "Judge people by consistent behaviour over time, not charm or words.",
      "Watch how someone treats people who can't do anything for them, like waiters, cleaners or junior staff. Be wary of flattery that comes too fast, stories where they're always the victim or the hero, rules that apply to everyone but them, and a lack of genuine apology. Notice how you feel after spending time with them. And be cautious about armchair diagnoses: calling people narcissists or psychopaths casually isn't fair or accurate, and only a qualified professional can assess a personality disorder. Focus on behaviour, keep your own boundaries clear, and give trust gradually as it's earned."],
  ],
  [
    [0, "In dark triad research, how are the three traits usually treated?", ["As rare mental illnesses", "As traits everyone has to some degree", "As proof of criminality", "As fixed from birth"], 1, "Most people score low to moderate; they are not clinical diagnoses."],
    [1, "Where does the word narcissism come from?", ["A Roman emperor", "A Greek youth who loved his own reflection", "A flower only", "A psychologist"], 1, "The flower is named after him too."],
    [2, "Who inspired the term Machiavellianism?", ["A modern CEO", "Niccolò Machiavelli, author of The Prince", "A Greek god", "A novel villain"], 1, "He advised it's safer to be feared than loved."],
    [3, "Roughly how common is clinical psychopathy in the general population?", ["About 1%", "About 25%", "About 50%", "0%"], 0, "Higher in prison populations."],
    [7, "What's the best way to judge someone's character?", ["Their charm", "Consistent behaviour over time, including towards people who can't help them", "Their job title", "First impressions"], 1, "Watch how they treat waiters."],
  ]);

WB.L("cults", "psychology", "intermediate", "How cults recruit",
  "Nobody joins a cult. People join a friendly group with answers, and the rest happens slowly.",
  [
    ["Not who you'd expect", "Cult members are often intelligent, educated and idealistic, not gullible.",
      "It's tempting to think only foolish or broken people join cults. Research and survivor accounts suggest otherwise: many members are educated, curious and idealistic, often recruited at transitional moments like starting university, moving cities, a breakup, a job loss or grief. At those times, people are looking for connection and direction. Cults don't advertise themselves as cults. They offer friendship, meaning, purpose and certainty, things everyone wants. Researchers today often prefer the term 'high-control group', because the same dynamics can appear in religious, political, wellness, business and even online communities. The pull is human, not stupid."],
    ["The recruitment funnel", "Groups start with warmth and small requests, then escalate commitment gradually.",
      "Recruitment typically starts with something harmless: a free course, a meditation class, a self-improvement seminar, a Bible study or a friendly dinner. New people get intense attention and affection, sometimes called love bombing. Requests start small, like attending another session, then grow: more time, more money, more loyalty. Each step feels like a small, reasonable extension of the last. Psychologists call this the foot-in-the-door technique: in a classic 1966 study, people who first agreed to a small request were much more likely to agree to a big one later. The full beliefs are often revealed only after people are deeply involved."],
    ["Control of information and time", "Cults often control members' time, information and relationships.",
      "Psychiatrist Robert Lifton, studying 'thought reform' in the 1950s, and later former member and counsellor Steven Hassan described patterns of control. Members' schedules fill with group activities, study and recruiting, leaving little time or energy to reflect. Outside information is portrayed as dangerous or corrupt. The group develops special jargon, which makes it harder to think in outside terms and signals who belongs. Contact with doubting family and friends is discouraged. Doubts are reframed as personal failings or attacks by evil forces. Hassan's model sums this up as control of behaviour, information, thoughts and emotions."],
    ["The leader", "Cults usually centre on a charismatic leader who claims special authority and accepts no criticism.",
      "Most high-control groups revolve around a leader claiming unique insight, divine connection or special knowledge. Questioning the leader is treated as betrayal. Rules may apply to members but not the leader, who may control members' money, relationships and even who they marry. History offers tragic examples: in 1978, more than 900 followers of Jim Jones's Peoples Temple died at Jonestown in Guyana, including many children. Australia has had its own, such as The Family, led by Anne Hamilton-Byrne, which operated near Melbourne from the 1960s and raised children in isolation under harsh conditions."],
    ["Why the techniques work", "Cults exploit ordinary mental habits: the drive for consistency, social proof and avoiding dissonance.",
      "None of these techniques need hypnosis or special powers. They lean on habits everyone has. We like to act consistently with past choices. We look to people around us to judge what's normal, especially in unfamiliar situations. And we hate the discomfort of holding beliefs that clash with our actions, which psychologist Leon Festinger called cognitive dissonance. In his 1956 book When Prophecy Fails, Festinger described a small group that predicted the world would end. When it didn't, some members became more committed, not less, and began recruiting harder. Admitting error would have meant admitting that their sacrifices were wasted."],
    ["Why people stay", "Sunk costs, fear, lost relationships and identity make leaving extremely hard.",
      "Leaving can mean losing your whole social world, your sense of purpose, and years invested, the sunk cost effect. Members may be told terrible things happen to those who leave, or that they'll be spiritually lost. Many have cut off family and have nowhere to go. Some have given the group their savings or worked unpaid for years, leaving them without money, qualifications or a work history. Children raised inside may know no other life. Cognitive dissonance also plays a role: the more someone has sacrificed, the stronger their need to believe it was worth it."],
    ["Warning signs", "Be cautious of groups that pressure, isolate, demand money or forbid questioning.",
      "Warning signs include pressure to commit quickly, a leader beyond criticism, us-versus-them thinking, secret teachings revealed only in stages, requests for large amounts of money or unpaid labour, discouraging contact with outsiders, and punishing doubt. Watch, too, for a group that decides members' sleep, diet, relationships or finances. None of these alone proves a group is harmful, but several together are a real concern. Healthy groups, including healthy religious communities, let you ask hard questions, take breaks, keep outside friends, see where money goes, and leave without being shunned or threatened."],
    ["Helping and getting help", "Stay warmly connected, ask questions rather than argue, and seek professional support.",
      "If someone you love is involved, staying warmly connected rather than arguing gives them a door back. Direct attacks on the group usually backfire, because members are often taught to expect exactly that from outsiders. Ask genuine questions, share memories of life before, and avoid ultimatums. Many people eventually leave on their own, often when doubts pile up, and a welcoming family makes that easier. Former members may need time to rebuild relationships, finances and trust in their own judgement, and a counsellor or GP is a good place to start. If there's coercive control or abuse, 1800RESPECT (1800 737 732) can help; in danger, call 000."],
  ],
  [
    [0, "When are people often recruited into cults?", ["Only in childhood", "At transitional moments like moving, grief or starting university", "When they're wealthy", "Only when they're religious"], 1, "The pull is human, not stupid."],
    [1, "How do cults typically escalate commitment?", ["Big demands up front", "Small requests that gradually grow", "Paying members", "Contracts"], 1, "Each step feels reasonable."],
    [2, "Why do cults develop special jargon?", ["For fun", "It makes it harder to think in outside terms", "To save time", "It's required by law"], 1, "Language shapes thought."],
    [5, "Why is leaving a cult so hard?", ["It's illegal", "Sunk costs, fear and loss of one's whole social world", "The doors are locked", "Members are happy"], 1, "Cognitive dissonance plays a role too."],
    [7, "How can you help someone in a cult?", ["Argue hard", "Stay warmly connected so they have a way back", "Cut them off", "Report them"], 1, "A door back matters."],
  ]);

WB.L("false-memories", "psychology", "intermediate", "False memories",
  "Your memory isn't a recording. It's a reconstruction, and it can be changed, or even invented, without you noticing.",
  [
    ["Memory rebuilds, it doesn't replay", "Each time you remember something, your brain reconstructs it and can change it.",
      "We imagine memory like a video we replay. In reality, the brain stores fragments, such as a sight, a feeling or the gist of what was said, and rebuilds the memory each time we recall it, filling gaps with expectations, later information and imagination. Every recall is a chance for the memory to shift. British psychologist Frederic Bartlett showed this in the 1930s: people retelling an unfamiliar folk tale gradually reshaped it to fit their own culture's expectations. That's why two people at the same event remember it differently, and why vivid, confident memories can still be wrong."],
    ["Remembering words you never saw", "Memory stores meaning, so people often 'recall' words that fit a theme but were never there.",
      "You can test reconstructive memory in a minute. In a method developed by James Deese and later Henry Roediger and Kathleen McDermott in 1995, people hear a list such as bed, rest, awake, tired, dream, snooze, blanket and nap. Later, many confidently remember hearing the word 'sleep', which was never on the list. Some even say they can picture it being read out. The brain stores the theme and fills in what 'should' be there. This harmless lab trick shows the same process behind more serious false memories: plausible details slot in smoothly and feel just as real as genuine ones."],
    ["The stop sign study", "Details slipped in after an event can quietly rewrite what people remember seeing.",
      "In a 1978 study, Elizabeth Loftus and colleagues showed people slides of a red car stopped at a stop sign before it turned and hit a pedestrian. Afterwards, some were asked a question that casually mentioned a 'yield' (give way) sign instead. When later shown both versions of the slide, people given the misleading question were far more likely to pick the one with the wrong sign, often confidently. This is called the misinformation effect: information received after an event can blend into the memory of the event itself. It has been reproduced many times and underpins how investigators are now trained to question witnesses."],
    ["Lost in the mall", "Researchers implanted false childhood memories in some participants through suggestion.",
      "In the 1990s, Loftus and Jacqueline Pickrell gave participants booklets describing childhood events supposedly supplied by relatives. One event, being lost in a shopping mall as a child, was invented. After repeated interviews, about a quarter of participants came to remember the false event, at least partly, some adding vivid details. Later studies implanted other false memories, such as a hot-air balloon ride shown in a doctored photo. Researchers still debate how many of these counted as full memories rather than vague beliefs, but the core finding holds: memory can be not only distorted, but fabricated."],
    ["Flashbulb memories", "Vivid memories of shocking events feel certain but are often inaccurate.",
      "Many people feel they remember exactly where they were when they heard shocking news, like the September 11 attacks in 2001. These are called flashbulb memories. After the 1986 Challenger space shuttle disaster, Ulric Neisser asked students how they'd heard, then asked again about two and a half years later; many accounts had changed dramatically. Researchers asked people the day after 9/11 how they heard, then asked again months later. Their stories changed considerably, yet their confidence stayed high. Vividness and confidence, it turns out, aren't reliable signs of accuracy."],
    ["Eyewitnesses and justice", "Mistaken eyewitness identification is a leading factor in wrongful convictions.",
      "Courts have long treated a confident eyewitness as powerful evidence. But the US Innocence Project found that mistaken eyewitness identification played a role in about 70% of convictions later overturned by DNA evidence. Stress, a weapon drawing attention, identifying someone of a different ethnic background, long delays and leading questions all reduce accuracy. Confidence also tends to grow after the fact, especially if police confirm a witness 'picked the right one'. Police and courts have since reformed line-up procedures, for example by having officers who don't know the suspect run them and recording the witness's confidence immediately."],
    ["The memory wars", "In the 1990s, fierce debate erupted over 'recovered' memories of abuse that emerged in therapy.",
      "In the 1980s and 1990s, some therapists used techniques like hypnosis, guided imagery and repeated suggestion to help clients 'recover' memories of childhood abuse they had no previous memory of. Some of these memories led to accusations and court cases, and some were later retracted. Researchers like Loftus argued that such techniques could create false memories, while others stressed that genuine abuse is real and often not disclosed for years. The bitter debate became known as the memory wars. It led many professional bodies to caution against suggestive therapy techniques, while affirming that survivors' accounts deserve to be taken seriously."],
    ["Living with a reconstructive memory", "Keep records, stay humble about certainty, and be careful with leading questions.",
      "Reconstructive memory isn't a flaw so much as a design: it lets us learn, generalise and imagine the future by recombining past experiences. But it means we should hold memories more lightly. Write important things down, such as what a doctor, landlord or employer told you. When disagreeing about what happened, remember both memories may be partly wrong, and neither person is necessarily lying. When asking others what they saw, especially children, use open questions like 'What happened next?' and avoid leading ones like 'Was the man wearing a red hat?'"],
  ],
  [
    [0, "How does the brain handle memories?", ["It stores perfect recordings", "It reconstructs them each time, and can change them", "It deletes them at night", "It stores them in the heart"], 1, "Every recall can shift a memory."],
    [2, "In the 1978 stop-sign study, what did a misleading question do?", ["Nothing measurable", "Led many people to 'remember' a give way sign that wasn't there", "Made people forget the whole slideshow", "Made their memories more accurate"], 1, "The misinformation effect."],
    [3, "What did the 'lost in the mall' study show?", ["Malls are dangerous", "False memories can be implanted by suggestion", "Children have perfect memory", "Memory can't change"], 1, "About a quarter came to remember the false event."],
    [4, "What's true of flashbulb memories?", ["They're perfectly accurate", "They feel certain but often change over time", "They only happen to children", "They're rare"], 1, "Confidence stays high even as details change."],
    [5, "What did the Innocence Project find?", ["Eyewitnesses are always right", "Mistaken eyewitness ID played a role in about 70% of DNA exonerations", "DNA is unreliable", "Juries ignore eyewitnesses"], 1, "Line-up procedures have since been reformed."],
  ]);

WB.L("optical-illusions", "psychology", "beginner", "Why optical illusions fool you",
  "Illusions aren't failures of your eyes. They're your brain's clever shortcuts, caught in the act.",
  [
    ["You see with your brain", "Vision is the brain's best guess about the world, built from limited information.",
      "Your eyes send the brain a flat, patchy, constantly shifting image. Only a small central area of each retina sees fine detail, and your eyes dart around several times a second. The brain turns this into a stable 3D world by making assumptions: light comes from above, objects keep their size, nearby edges belong together. These shortcuts work so well that we don't notice them. Optical illusions are cleverly designed situations where the assumptions give the wrong answer. They're valuable to scientists precisely because they reveal how much of seeing is interpretation rather than simple recording."],
    ["The Müller-Lyer illusion", "Two equal lines look different lengths depending on the arrows at their ends.",
      "In the Müller-Lyer illusion, first published by Franz Müller-Lyer in 1889, a line with fins angled outward from its ends looks longer than an identical line capped with ordinary arrowheads. One theory is that the brain reads the shapes as corners: the arrowhead version like the near outside corner of a building, the fins version like the far inside corner of a room, and it adjusts perceived size for distance. Researchers in the 1960s reported that people from some cultures with fewer straight-edged buildings were less fooled, though this finding and its explanation are still debated."],
    ["Shadows and colour", "The brain adjusts perceived colour for lighting, which can make identical colours look different.",
      "In Edward Adelson's 1995 checker-shadow illusion, a square in shadow looks much lighter than a square in light, though they're exactly the same shade on the page. The brain corrects for shadows so objects keep a consistent colour, which is useful in real life: a white shirt still looks white under a tree. The same process, called colour constancy, explains 'the dress', a 2015 internet photo some people saw as blue and black and others as white and gold. The dress was actually blue and black; viewers disagreed depending on what lighting their brain assumed."],
    ["The moon illusion", "The moon looks bigger near the horizon, though its image in your eye is the same size.",
      "A rising full moon can look enormous, yet high in the sky it seems small. Photographs show the moon's image is essentially the same size in both positions; if anything it is very slightly smaller at the horizon, because it's a little further from you. The illusion has been discussed since ancient times, and there's still no single agreed explanation. Leading ideas involve the brain treating the horizon as further away than the sky overhead, so it scales the moon up, and comparisons with trees and buildings nearby. Try viewing a horizon moon through a rolled-up tube: it often shrinks."],
    ["Moving pictures that don't move", "Some static images seem to move because of how the eye and brain process contrast and motion.",
      "In illusions like Japanese researcher Akiyoshi Kitaoka's 'rotating snakes', a still image appears to swirl. Researchers think the effect comes from tiny eye movements and the way brain cells respond at slightly different speeds to light and dark areas, producing a false motion signal. It's weaker when you stare at one spot. Cinema relies on a related trick: still frames shown in quick succession, usually 24 per second for film, are perceived as smooth movement. Another is the waterfall illusion: stare at flowing water, then look at the rocks, and they seem to drift upward."],
    ["Ambiguous figures", "Some images can be seen two ways, and the brain flips between them.",
      "Look at the famous Rubin vase, described by Danish psychologist Edgar Rubin around 1915, and you see either a vase or two faces in profile, but it's hard to see both at once. The duck-rabbit and the Necker cube, a wireframe cube whose front face seems to flip, work the same way. The image on your retina never changes; only your interpretation does. The brain picks one reading and commits to it, then switches. This shows perception isn't passive: the brain actively chooses what you see, and knowing the trick doesn't stop the flipping."],
    ["Your blind spot", "Each eye has a blind spot with no light receptors, and the brain fills it in.",
      "Where the optic nerve leaves the back of each eye, there are no light-sensitive cells, creating a blind spot. It's not tiny: it covers an area of your visual field several times wider than the full moon appears. You never notice it, partly because each eye covers the other's gap, and partly because the brain fills the hole with whatever surrounds it, such as the colour of a wall or the pattern of a carpet. You can find yours by closing one eye and moving a small dot across a page until it vanishes. The brain invents the missing piece without telling you."],
    ["Illusions beyond sight", "Your other senses and your judgement have illusions too.",
      "The McGurk effect, reported in 1976, shows that what you see changes what you hear: a video of lips saying 'ga' combined with audio of 'ba' often sounds like 'da'. In the size-weight illusion, a small object feels heavier than a large one of the same weight, because we expect the big one to be heavier. Food served on a different coloured plate can even taste different. Cognitive biases are, in a sense, illusions of thinking. The lesson from all illusions: feeling certain about what you perceive doesn't guarantee it's accurate, which is worth remembering whenever you're sure you saw something."],
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why do optical illusions work?", ["Our eyes are broken", "They exploit the brain's shortcuts for interpreting what we see", "They use magic", "They only work on screens"], 1, "Seeing is interpretation."],
    [1, "In the Müller-Lyer illusion, what's actually true of the two lines?", ["One is longer", "They're the same length", "They're different colours", "They're curved"], 1, "The arrowheads fool us."],
    [2, "What explains 'the dress' disagreement?", ["Colour blindness", "Different assumptions about the lighting", "Bad screens", "A hoax"], 1, "The brain corrects for light."],
    [5, "What is the Rubin vase?", ["A Greek artefact", "An image that can be seen as a vase or two faces", "A magic trick", "A painting technique"], 1, "The brain flips between interpretations."],
    [7, "What does the McGurk effect show?", ["We hear with our eyes closed", "What you see changes what you hear", "Music affects mood", "Loud noises hurt"], 1, "'Ga' plus 'ba' can sound like 'da'."],
  ]);

WB.L("dunning-kruger", "psychology", "beginner", "The Dunning–Kruger effect",
  "Why the least skilled are sometimes the most confident, why experts often doubt themselves, and why the famous graph is a myth.",
  [
    ["The original study", "In 1999, Dunning and Kruger found low performers greatly overestimated their ability.",
      "Psychologists David Dunning and Justin Kruger, at Cornell University, tested students on logical reasoning, grammar and humour, then asked them to estimate how well they did compared with peers. Those who scored in the bottom quarter, around the 12th percentile on average, estimated they were around the 62nd percentile, comfortably above average. Top performers, meanwhile, slightly underestimated themselves. The paper's title summed it up: 'Unskilled and unaware of it'. Dunning has said he was partly inspired by a 1995 news story about a bank robber who believed lemon juice on his face would hide him from security cameras."],
    ["The double burden", "The skills you need to do something well are often the skills needed to judge how well you did it.",
      "Dunning's explanation is a double burden. If you don't know grammar well, you can't write well, and you also can't recognise good or bad grammar, including your own mistakes. Incompetence hides itself. A new chess player may not see why a move is terrible; a novice investor may not know what they don't know; a learner driver may not notice the hazards an experienced driver scans for automatically. Experts, by contrast, can see the full complexity of their field, so they're more aware of what could go wrong and how much they still don't know."],
    ["Why experts underestimate", "Skilled people often assume tasks that are easy for them are easy for everyone.",
      "Top performers in the study didn't badly misjudge their own scores; they misjudged others, assuming everyone found the task as easy as they did. When shown other people's answers, they revised their estimates upward, while low performers generally didn't. This is part of a broader pattern called the curse of knowledge: once you know something well, it's hard to imagine not knowing it. That's why experts can be poor teachers, skipping steps that seem obvious to them but aren't obvious to beginners, and why instruction manuals written by engineers can baffle everyone else."],
    ["The Mount Stupid myth", "The popular 'Mount Stupid' graph isn't from the research and misrepresents it.",
      "Online, the effect is often shown as a curve where beginners' confidence rockets to a peak nicknamed 'Mount Stupid', crashes into a 'valley of despair', then slowly climbs with expertise. That graph wasn't in the original study, and it doesn't match the data. In Dunning and Kruger's results, confidence rose steadily with actual skill; it just rose far less steeply than skill did. Low performers didn't think they were experts, only that they were a bit above average. The meme is a catchy story about learning, but it's a later internet invention, not a research finding."],
    ["The statistical critique", "Part of the pattern may be a statistical artefact, and the true effect may be smaller than claimed.",
      "Some researchers argue that much of the classic pattern would appear even if people had no special blind spot. Two things combine. Most people rate themselves somewhat above average, the better-than-average effect. And self-estimates are noisy, so they drift back towards the middle, known as regression to the mean. Together these guarantee that low scorers seem to overestimate and high scorers to underestimate. Studies in 2016 and 2020 using random or simulated data reproduced much of the famous chart. Debate continues: some find a real but smaller effect, while the core idea that self-assessment is often inaccurate is widely supported."],
    ["It applies to everyone", "Everyone is a novice in most areas, so everyone is vulnerable to overconfidence somewhere.",
      "The Dunning–Kruger effect isn't about 'stupid people'. Dunning himself stresses that it applies to all of us in the many areas where we're beginners. A brilliant doctor can be overconfident about investing; a skilled engineer about nutrition; a seasoned lawyer about home renovation. Overconfidence in general is well documented: in a well-known 1981 study, most American student drivers rated themselves safer and more skilful than the median driver, which can't be true of most people. The first step in guarding against it is admitting that it applies to you, not just to people you disagree with."],
    ["Where it shows up", "Overconfidence matters most where mistakes are costly and feedback is slow.",
      "Misjudging your own skill is harmless in a pub trivia night but costly elsewhere. Beginner investors who feel confident may trade too often, which research links to lower returns. Online, people with a little knowledge can confidently contradict specialists, and confident voices tend to get shared more widely. In workplaces, confident people are often assumed to be competent, so overconfidence can be rewarded in the short term. The risk is greatest where feedback is slow or unclear, such as health choices, long-term finances or complex projects, because reality doesn't correct you quickly. That's where checking your judgement matters most."],
    ["Staying calibrated", "Seek feedback, test your knowledge, and learn enough to see what you don't know.",
      "You can improve your self-assessment. Ask for honest feedback from people who know the field, and listen when it's uncomfortable. Test yourself rather than just feeling confident: explain an idea out loud, try a practice question, or predict an outcome and check it later. Learning more tends to reduce overconfidence because it reveals the size of the field. For important decisions, get a second opinion from a qualified professional. And cultivate intellectual humility: saying 'I'm not sure' or 'I might be wrong' is a sign of wisdom, as Socrates suggested about 2,400 years ago."],
  ],
  [
    [0, "What did Dunning and Kruger find about the lowest scorers?", ["They underestimated themselves", "They greatly overestimated their ability", "They guessed accurately", "They refused to answer"], 1, "Bottom scorers placed themselves above average."],
    [1, "What is the 'double burden'?", ["Having two jobs", "Lacking a skill also means lacking the ability to judge it", "Being tired and hungry", "Learning two languages"], 1, "Incompetence hides itself."],
    [2, "Why did top performers underestimate themselves?", ["They were modest", "They assumed tasks easy for them were easy for everyone", "They didn't try", "They cheated"], 1, "The curse of knowledge."],
    [3, "Was the 'Mount Stupid' graph in the original study?", ["Yes", "No, it's a later internet addition", "Only in the sequel", "It was the title"], 1, "Confidence actually rose steadily with skill."],
    [7, "What helps you stay well calibrated?", ["Avoiding feedback", "Seeking feedback and testing your knowledge", "Trusting confidence", "Reading headlines"], 1, "Intellectual humility helps."],
  ]);

WB.L("intuition", "psychology", "intermediate", "When to trust your gut",
  "Sometimes gut instinct beats careful analysis. Sometimes it's dangerously wrong. Here's how to tell the difference.",
  [
    ["Two ways of thinking", "Psychologists describe fast intuitive thinking (System 1) and slow deliberate thinking (System 2).",
      "Psychologist Daniel Kahneman, who won the 2002 Nobel prize in economics, popularised the idea of two systems in his 2011 book Thinking, Fast and Slow. System 1 is fast, automatic and effortless: recognising a face, sensing anger in a voice, knowing 2 plus 2. System 2 is slow, effortful and deliberate: working out 17 times 24, parallel parking in a tight spot, or weighing a big decision. Intuition is System 1 at work. It handles most of daily life brilliantly, but it also produces predictable biases. The two systems are a helpful metaphor, not two separate parts of the brain."],
    ["Intuition is pattern recognition", "Expert intuition is fast recognition of patterns learned through experience.",
      "Psychologist Gary Klein studied firefighters who made split-second decisions. In one case he described, a commander ordered his crew out of a house moments before the floor collapsed; he couldn't explain why at the time. Later analysis suggested he'd unconsciously noticed that the fire was too quiet and the room too hot for a small kitchen fire: it was actually burning in the basement below. His 'gut feeling' was years of experience recognising patterns faster than conscious thought. Herbert Simon, another Nobel laureate, put it simply: intuition is nothing more and nothing less than recognition."],
    ["When intuition works", "Intuition is reliable in predictable environments with fast, clear feedback.",
      "Kahneman and Klein, who started with opposite views, wrote a joint paper in 2009 agreeing on when to trust intuition. It works when the environment is regular enough to have learnable patterns, and when the person has had lots of practice with quick, clear feedback. Chess players, experienced nurses, firefighters, pilots and athletes fit this. So does an experienced tradie who can hear that an engine or pump isn't right. Your gut about whether a close friend is upset is often good too, after years of practice reading them. The key question is always where the intuition was learned."],
    ["When it fails", "Intuition is unreliable in unpredictable fields or where feedback is slow or missing.",
      "In some fields, experience doesn't build good intuition because outcomes are too random or feedback too delayed. Stock pickers, political pundits and some long-range forecasters show little genuine intuitive skill, even though they feel confident. Philip Tetlock's long-running research on expert predictions, published in 2005, found many experts barely beat chance on long-term political forecasts, and often did worse than simple statistical rules. Famous experts did no better than obscure ones. Feeling certain is not evidence of accuracy: confidence comes from how coherent a story feels, and confidence and correctness can come apart."],
    ["Shortcuts and biases", "Intuition relies on mental shortcuts that are usually useful but sometimes systematically wrong.",
      "In 1974, Amos Tversky and Kahneman described heuristics, mental shortcuts intuition relies on. The availability heuristic judges how common something is by how easily examples come to mind, which is why vivid shark attacks or plane crashes feel more likely than they are. Anchoring means an initial number, even a random one, pulls later estimates towards it, which is why an asking price shapes what a house seems worth. These biases are well replicated. Not every famous finding held up, though: some studies Kahneman cited on subtle 'priming' failed to replicate, and he publicly acknowledged relying on them too heavily."],
    ["Gut feelings about people", "Intuitions about people can be useful warning signs, but can also reflect bias.",
      "A feeling that something is 'off' about a situation or person can be your brain picking up real cues: inconsistent stories, body language, pressure tactics, or someone ignoring a small 'no'. Safety experts often advise not to talk yourself out of such feelings when your safety is involved. But gut reactions to people are also shaped by stereotypes, accents, appearance and first impressions, which can be unfair and inaccurate. Job interviews are a classic example: unstructured chats often feel insightful but predict performance less well than structured questions. Treat a gut feeling about someone as a prompt to look more closely, not as a final verdict."],
    ["Training better intuition", "Intuition improves with deliberate practice, honest feedback and keeping score.",
      "Good intuition isn't simply a gift; it can be trained where feedback exists. Experts build it through thousands of cases where they made a judgement and found out quickly whether they were right. You can borrow the method: write down predictions before you learn the outcome, then check them. Tetlock's later Good Judgment Project, run from 2011, found that ordinary volunteers who did this carefully, updated their views often and thought in probabilities, became strikingly accurate forecasters, dubbed 'superforecasters'. Short training in reasoning about probabilities also improved accuracy. Keeping score turns vague hunches into skill you can actually measure."],
    ["Using both systems well", "Use intuition as data, and slow down for big, unfamiliar or high-stakes decisions.",
      "A practical approach: notice your gut reaction, then ask whether you have real experience in this kind of situation with clear feedback. If yes, give your intuition weight. If the decision is big, unfamiliar or emotionally charged, slow down: write out options, consider what would change your mind, and ask others, including a qualified professional for health, money or legal matters. Kahneman himself suggested delaying intuition: gather the facts first, then form your overall judgement. Another approach is to make an intuitive guess, do the analysis, and compare the two. Where they disagree, you've learned something worth examining."],
  ],
  [
    [0, "What is System 1 thinking?", ["Slow and deliberate", "Fast, automatic and intuitive", "Computer processing", "Group decisions"], 1, "System 2 is slow and effortful."],
    [1, "Why did the firefighter order his crew out?", ["He was scared", "He unconsciously recognised signs the fire was below them", "A radio warning", "Luck"], 1, "Intuition is pattern recognition."],
    [2, "When is intuition most reliable?", ["In random environments", "In predictable environments with fast, clear feedback", "When you're tired", "When you feel certain"], 1, "Kahneman and Klein agreed on this."],
    [3, "What did Tetlock find about expert long-term political forecasts?", ["They're highly accurate", "Many barely beat chance", "They're always wrong", "Only economists succeed"], 1, "Confidence isn't accuracy."],
    [7, "What's wise for a big, unfamiliar decision?", ["Go with your gut immediately", "Slow down and analyse, using intuition as one input", "Flip a coin", "Let others decide"], 1, "Use both systems."],
  ]);
